t have them, they sure as hell won't cede them to those dirty foreigners. And there, I think, it's up to everyone themselves to decide where to place the Egyptian legacy in a Scion setting; Scion is still fictional, we're allowed to make stuff up. It was far easier drawing modern connections in the Shen and Teotl Previews, because both religions and mythologies are still carried on in a myriad ways in the modern day. That isn't the case for the Egyptians. In contrast to the Greeks and Romans, the Egyptians really were completely forgotten in both Medieval Europe and the Muslim Arab Empires. Their monuments were buried in sand, and their writing became unintelligible.The modern Egyptian opinion seems divided between laying claim to that great legacy, and discarding it for their even greater Islamic legacy, and many poor people just wanting to go with whatever the tourists will pay for. There are modern revivals like Kemeticism, but try as hard as they may, that doesn't create a continuous line of tradition between them and Ramesses. That line is just lost, in a way the Greek, Chinese, or Mexican lines are not. That's why I, personally, with no claim to that idea being generally binding, prefer the approach of &quot;Egypt is everywhere&quot;. Because not only since the 19th century, not only since we adore the Egyptian's greatness in museums, but for a long time, through all those cultures that have borrowed and copied from the great Egyptian legacy, Egyptian ideas have spread all over Europe, Africa, and the Near East. So that is something I could go with - the Chinese and Aztec gods being kept by what remains of their cults, while the Egyptian gods, whose proper cult is lost for good, being carried on in all of our minds. (That is, again, not to tear down Kemetics; I'm merely saying that whatever honest and pious devotions they pay to their gods, those are not equivalent to what the Netjer received in the old days; Egyptian religion was a state cult, and with that state gone, there can be substitute cults, but never again the &quot;real deal&quot;.)<br /> <br /> Now, that was one massive <i>tour de force </i>through some really big questions. I'm still pretty sure I haven't answered what you actually wanted to know, but I hope this has gone some steps in the right direction.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Yes, I am new, so thank you for the interesting outline between the two. I have read far more from various people about most of this, some who might agree and some who might disagree with all the points you made. So many like to compare these two and their influence in the world today, and while I understand certain positions have more evidence to back them up, I find the conversation more interesting than the winner of an argument. This is also what I had decided to do when I studied under my father on our own culture, or when I had studied the Japanese and Indian systems of knowledge. I am just not interested in the outcome of what the truth is because it never can be absolute, I am interested in the conversation. I didn't like the conversation that was made out of the Egyptian gods. I didn't know why at the time, so I made efforts to determine that. I am quite happy about what I read in this thread because it seems I am not mistaken about the contentions, which is why I started this thread in the first place. So thank you everyone for what you said here.<br /> <br /> <br /> Regarding that Black Athena question, I will look into it since it seems to add even more stuff to the conversation, and the more the better.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>How can I know if what I claim I know to be true is rejecting the idea that there is something I might not know? How can I know if what I claim I don't know to be true is rejecting the idea that there is something I do know?<br /> -Zhuangzi","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpat